  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 150 AM MDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nA pattern shift is currently underway that will lead to chances for \nprecipitation each day through the rest of the week and through the \nweekend. This shift will also put an end to the latest heat wave \nwith temperatures remaining at or slightly below normal through the \nweekend. The culprits for this cooler and wetter pattern will be a \nbroad area of high pressure over the Deep South/Gulf Coast and a \nlongwave trough over the West Coast. The Cowboy State will be \nsandwiched between these two features resulting in a moist \nsouthwesterly flow pattern. Embedded shortwaves will rotate \nnorthward over the region, bringing widespread chances for showers \nand thunderstorms across the CWA each day through Sunday. The threat \nfor locally heavy rain will also return as monsoonal moisture will \nreturn as well. Precipitable water values of 0.75\" to 1\" will be in \nplace across the region today and remain in place through the \nforecast period. Chances for precipitation will increase over \nwestern portions after sunrise this morning, spreading across areas \neast of the Divide through the rest of the morning and continue \nthrough the rest of the day. A separate wave of showers looks \nto move over Sweetwater County after 20Z. Most showers will be \nending in the evening, with some lingering over the central \nbasins through 08Z Thursday. Any thunderstorm is likely to be \nvery isolated due to a lack of instability for the mostly \ncloudy/overcast skies. These clouds will also be the reason why \ntemperatures will be 4 to 10 degrees cooler today. The \nhazy/smoky conditions will begin to thin today, with improving \nconditions expected by Thursday as the increased chances for \nprecipitation allow the atmosphere to become more mixed. \nAdditionally, the southwest flow aloft will cut off the source \nregion of most of the smoke (the large fire complexes in OR/WA).\n\n\nShowers and possible thunderstorms will return across areas west of \nthe Divide by early Thursday afternoon and spread across the rest of \nthe CWA through the rest of the day into the evening. There will \nstill be a threat for locally heavy rain, with a higher focus over \nJohnson County and portions of Natrona County. This will be due to \nGulf moisture advecting into the region, pushing PWAT values to \n1.25\" in this area. Strong thunderstorms are possible as well, with \nforecasted CAPE values of 1500 to 2000 J/kg and a lifted index of \nminus 4 to minus 6. However localized flash flooding will likely be \nthe highest threat as bulk shear will only be up to 30kt. WPC \nExcessive Rain Outlook is also forecasting a Slight risk for\nthis area for Thursday.",
